I'll review EVAL-04 and build a threat model based on the payment design described.

Analyzing the context, I can see a critical security flaw: the server trusts a client-side "purchased" flag to unlock premium features. This is a fundamental vulnerability in payment processing.

Here's the threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-04",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Mobile app", "/premium/unlock API", "Premium service", "User account database"],
    "dataStores": ["User accounts", "Premium entitlements"],
    "externalServices": ["Apple App Store", "Google Play Store"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Mobile app to backend server"],
    "dataClasses": ["payment"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Client-controlled flag bypasses payment verification",
      "scenario": "An attacker intercepts the POST /premium/unlock request and sets \"purchased\": true without completing a real purchase through the app store. The server accepts this client-provided flag without validation and unlocks premium features, allowing the attacker to access paid features for free, causing direct revenue loss.",
      "component": "/premium/unlock API endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The server trusts the client flag \"purchased\": true and unlocks premium for the account."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V8.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-602"],
      "plainLanguage": "Anyone can unlock premium features without paying by modifying the request to claim they purchased when they didn't",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate purchases server-side with app store",
        "given": "a POST request to /premium/unlock",
        "when": "processing the premium unlock",
        "then": "verify the receipt with Apple/Google APIs and ignore the client's 'purchased' flag"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Receipt not validated with app store APIs",
      "scenario": "The design mentions receiving a receipt but only checks a client flag. An attacker can forge a fake receipt or modify a real one, as the server never validates the receipt signature or authenticity with Apple or Google. This allows unlimited free premium access through crafted receipts.",
      "component": "/premium/unlock API endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app calls POST /premium/unlock with the receipt. The server trusts the client flag \"purchased\": true"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-345"],
      "plainLanguage": "The server doesn't check if receipts are real by asking Apple or Google, so attackers can make up fake receipts",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify receipt signatures with platform APIs",
        "given": "a receipt in the unlock request",
        "when": "processing premium activation",
        "then": "validate receipt signature with Apple's verifyReceipt or Google's purchase verification API before granting access"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Receipt reuse unlocks premium on multiple accounts",
      "scenario": "Without binding receipts to specific user accounts, an attacker with one valid purchase receipt can unlock premium on unlimited accounts by reusing the same receipt. They can share the receipt with friends or sell premium access, multiplying revenue loss from a single purchase.",
      "component": "/premium/unlock API endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app calls POST /premium/unlock with the receipt"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-294"],
      "plainLanguage": "One person's purchase receipt can be copied and used to unlock premium on many different accounts",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Bind receipts to user accounts",
        "given": "a receipt validation succeeds",
        "when": "activating premium",
        "then": "verify the receipt's original transaction user matches the requesting account and mark receipt as used"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Users keep premium after app store refunds",
      "scenario": "A user purchases premium, unlocks it via the endpoint, then requests a refund from Apple or Google. Without server-side validation or webhook handling for refunds, the server never learns about the refund, leaving premium active indefinitely while the company loses the payment.",
      "component": "Premium service",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The server trusts the client flag \"purchased\": true and unlocks premium for the account."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-345"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users can get their money back from Apple or Google but keep premium features because the system doesn't track refunds",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Handle app store refund notifications",
        "given": "premium is active for an account",
        "when": "Apple or Google issues a refund",
        "then": "receive webhook notification and immediately revoke premium access"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

